## Why This Is Asked
Tests fundamental React Native bridging concepts, platform-specific API integration, and event stream management with proper lifecycle handling.

## Key Concepts
- React Native native modules and TurboModule architecture
- Platform APIs: iOS UIDevice battery monitoring, Android BatteryManager
- Event emitters and lifecycle cleanup
- Throttling strategies to preserve battery life

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example JS usage
import { NativeModules, NativeEventEmitter } from 'react-native';
const { BatteryMonitor } = NativeModules;
const emitter = new NativeEventEmitter(BatteryMonitor);

// Initialize monitor
BatteryMonitor.initMonitor({ intervalMs: 5000 });

// Listen for battery changes
const subscription = emitter.addListener('onBatteryChange', (batteryInfo) => {
  console.log('Battery level:', batteryInfo.level);
  console.log('Is charging:', batteryInfo.isCharging);
});

// Start monitoring
BatteryMonitor.startMonitoring();

// Get current battery state
const currentBattery = BatteryMonitor.getCurrentBattery();

// Stop monitoring and cleanup
BatteryMonitor.stopMonitoring();
subscription.remove();
```

## Platform Differences
**iOS**: Uses UIDevice notifications, requires enabling battery monitoring, provides state change notifications automatically.

**Android**: Uses broadcast receivers for battery changes, requires explicit polling with timers, more granular battery information available.

## Power Implications
- Frequent polling drains battery; use appropriate intervals
- iOS notifications are event-driven and more efficient
- Android requires careful background task management
- Consider throttling updates and cleanup on app backgrounding

## Why This Is Asked

Tests offline-first sync design, conflict resolution, and native bridge considerations. Evaluates real-world trade-offs between consistency models and performance.

## Key Concepts

- **Offline-first sync architecture**: Prioritizes local-first writes with eventual consistency to remote
- **TurboModule/JSI bridges**: Enables synchronous, type-safe native communication without bridge serialization overhead
- **Local SQLite queue**: Tracks pending changes with timestamps and operation types for reliable sync
- **Conflict resolution strategies**: Version vectors for causality tracking; CRDT or last-write-wins for merge semantics
- **Schema migrations**: Additive migrations with backward compatibility; migration scripts stored in versioned history table
- **Background workers**: Dedicated threads prevent UI jank during heavy I/O operations
- **Backpressure handling**: Bounded queue sizes with configurable flush thresholds

## Code Example

```javascript
import { startSync, pauseSync, resumeSync, stopSync, getSyncStatus } from '@app/sync';

await startSync('https://api.example.com/sync', {
  pollMs: 30000,
  backoff: { initial: 1000, max: 60000, jitter: 0.3 },
  onRecordSynced: (record) => console.log('Synced:', record.id),
  onRemoteChange: (changes) => applyRemoteChanges(changes),
});

// UI indicator
const status = getSyncStatus();
console.log(`Pending: ${status.pending}, Last sync: ${status.lastSyncAt}`);
```

